      "rationale": "The reported IndexError comes from sqlparse/filters/output.py's OutputPHPFilter._process, which on encountering a newline token does `token.value.split('\\n', 1)[2]` to compute the indentation to carry over to the next line; since str.split(sep, 1) returns at most 2 elements, index 2 is always out of range, so ANY multi-line statement crashes when output_format='php' is used (this is a pure regression versus the sibling OutputPythonFilter a few lines above, which correctly uses index [1] for the exact same purpose). The first test reproduces this crash with a minimal two-line SELECT and pins down the correct post-fix output, derived by hand-tracing the filter's own token-emission logic (header tokens, quote alignment via the extra space before '=', and the '.=' continuation line) using index [1] instead of [2] \u2014 i.e. the behavior the surrounding code (and the parallel python-format code path) clearly intends. The second test locks in the already-correct single-line case so the fix can't be verified by accidentally breaking that path.",
